{"questions":[{"question_id":"1l2kmhdub","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2011 Paper 1 Offline","year":2011,"examDate":2011,"subject":"physics","chapter":"rotational-motion","chapterGroup":"mechanics","topic":"torque","type":"mcqm","marks":4,"negMarks":2,"permalink":"pa-metal-rod-of-length-l-and-mass-m-is-pivoted-at-one-end-jee-advanced-physics-units-and-measurements-5fbzjlmj9jdaqsbw","content":"<p>A metal rod of length L and mass m is pivoted at one end. A thin disk of mass M and radius R ( &lt; L) is attached at its centre to the free end of the rod. Consider two ways the disc is attached : (case A). The disc is not free to rotate about its centre and (case B) the disc is free to rotate about its centre. The rod-disc system performs SHM in vertical plane after being released from the same displaced position. Which of the following statement(s) is(are) true?</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1l2lyk7cz/fe58dca4-7769-4cc5-bce7-8ca117e610fc/aa9cf930-c891-11ec-9a3d-695daad02a0a/file-1l2lyk7d0.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/1l2lyk7cz/fe58dca4-7769-4cc5-bce7-8ca117e610fc/aa9cf930-c891-11ec-9a3d-695daad02a0a/file-1l2lyk7d0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0px auto;max-height:40vh\" alt=\"IIT-JEE 2011 Paper 1 Offline Physics - Rotational Motion Question 35 English\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/1l2lyk7cz/fe58dca4-7769-4cc5-bce7-8ca117e610fc/aa9cf930-c891-11ec-9a3d-695daad02a0a/file-1l2lyk7d0.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/1l2lyk7cz/fe58dca4-7769-4cc5-bce7-8ca117e610fc/aa9cf930-c891-11ec-9a3d-695daad02a0a/file-1l2lyk7d0.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1l2lyk7cz/fe58dca4-7769-4cc5-bce7-8ca117e610fc/aa9cf930-c891-11ec-9a3d-695daad02a0a/file-1l2lyk7d0.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/1l2lyk7cz/fe58dca4-7769-4cc5-bce7-8ca117e610fc/aa9cf930-c891-11ec-9a3d-695daad02a0a/file-1l2lyk7d0.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/1l2lyk7cz/fe58dca4-7769-4cc5-bce7-8ca117e610fc/aa9cf930-c891-11ec-9a3d-695daad02a0a/file-1l2lyk7d0.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/1l2lyk7cz/fe58dca4-7769-4cc5-bce7-8ca117e610fc/aa9cf930-c891-11ec-9a3d-695daad02a0a/file-1l2lyk7d0.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"></p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Restoring torque in case A = Restoring torque in case B."},{"identifier":"B","content":"Restoring torque in case A < Restoring torque in case B."},{"identifier":"C","content":"Angular frequency for case A > Angular frequency for case B."},{"identifier":"D","content":"Angular frequency for case A < Angular frequency for case B."}],"correct_options":["A","D"],"explanation":"<p>We have</p>\n<p>Restoring torque = Force of gravity on disc and rod which is same in both cases.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0.124ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.131ex\" height=\"0.88ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -444 500 389\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2219\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2219\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> Case A : The moment of inertia is</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.816ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24.003ex\" height=\"3.041ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -983.7 10609.1 1344.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=